Cell["\<\
We now have a working program.  We can now write the final function \
ComplexMap[].  However, you see that our own code is fairly complex.  If we \
use the built-in function ParametricPlot[], we can do it much simpler.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
MATHEMATICA
PROGRAMMING\
\>", "Title"],

Cell["Plotting the complex function, continued", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
Previously, we have programmed the ComplexMap with straight line segments.  \
This picture is faithful only at the intersection points of two perpendicular \
lines.  The second, and better solution is to use smooth curves, generated by \
ParametricPlot[].   The mapping then is faithfully represented for all the \
lines in the pre-image grids.  First, let us see how to draw a single line by \
ParametricPlot:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(y0\  = \ 0.0;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(ParametricPlot[{x, y0}, \ {x, \ 0, 1}]\)}], "Input"],

